george eustis is here with us now  environment secretary of course i think  i might say you're supporting grant  shaps that's right yes i think he would  take part in our debate i'm sure he  would i mean as you know grant shaps has  never been shy in terms of coming out  all the media rounds often on difficult  days in fact one of the reasons i'm  supporting him  is firstly i've seen him in cabinet over  the last two years and i think he's  shown a good judgement i've often been  in cobra meetings with him and we've had  for instance issues around  the short straights and transport and  getting goods into the country and i  think he's always dealt with what's  thrown his way very very well but also  he's always been willing to go out and  uh back for government on difficult days  when we have those uh difficult times  i've i've always done that he has too so  i'm sure he would take part in that  debate yes why him  over others because i mean it's fair to  say he's not a frontrunner is he well  look i've always taken the view uh in  leadership  campaigns and this is the fourth one  that i've seen now that actually what  you should do is uh decide who you think  is the best candidate out of the field  and there are many good talented  candidates they've all got their  strengths but my view is that grant  shaps is the best man to become our  leader and our prime minister at this  particular point he's got cabinet  experience over uh um several different  leaders he's shown very good judgments  uh in my view  he's a very good communicator he did a  stint as party chairman so he's a good  campaigner and we're going to need  someone who can campaign as we go into  the next uh next general election so i  just think he's got all of the strengths  and the experience that we need at this  particular time and that's why i'm  backing him who else would you fold in  behind  i'm not getting into that i generally  take the view that i pick the candidate  that i think should win and i give it my  all to help them win uh if something  goes wrong along the way well then i'll  probably uh you know privately decide  what to do at that point but at the  moment uh we're talking to lots of  people we're getting lots of interest in  his campaign  i think he's only just launched  yesterday but i think he's a very very  strong candidate and i hope that we can  get him that through to that final  run-off for party members looks like  it's already getting ugly  well we tend to get this sort of uh uh  joshing during any any sort of  leadership campaign as i said the first  one i did was when we got david cameron  elected it was a little bit similar then  but i he came through the field though  didn't he i mean he wasn't a  front-runner uh that's all right he  wasn't a front-runner and that's why i  don't believe um any mps should sit on  their hands and wait to see which way  the wind's blowing or declare early  because they think someone's going to  win and they just want to  show their support for who they deem to  be the probable winner it's always the  right thing to do  to make sure that you put your support  behind the candidate that you believe in  and not be shy of coming out publicly  and saying so in the first round and  that's why i'm doing it now for grant  shaps because i think he's the best  candidate  i'm not going to sit on my hands and say  well who might win and who might be in  the final round and will he get support  i'm going to get out there and help him  get that support because i'm confident  he's the right person to do it seems a  bit unseemly doesn't it 12.  am i 11 so far probably 12 when pretty  patel um maybe throws a hat into the  ring later on today how are you going to  whittle it down well it is a very wide  field there's no doubt about that and um  the party does have a process for for  whittling it down so there will they  every candidate will need to get you  know a small number of mps to get  nominated and then at home and it used  to be eight i think wasn't it i think it  has been 12 it could be 30. well no it  will be for the 1922 committee to decide  the rules i suspect that they will keep  at the numbers for nominations uh you  know reasonably low so that everybody  gets a chance  well i don't know it's not i'm not on  the 1922 committee they'll decide but  but i would read reports in the paper  that it could be up to 30 you don't  think it'll be that um well i don't  think it should be that because i think  actually what they um what everyone  deserves is a run at the leadership in  the first round whether they then have a  threshold above which you've got to get  in that first round i think that's a  different matter so we might see several  candidates locked out in the first round  and then of course the the exhaustive  ballot process as it's called where you  just keep eliminating uh the final  runner and their votes get redistributed  to the next round that can be done  actually very quickly you can condense  that into a few days if you need to you  do need to we will need to um because i  i think um it's generally accepted that  we should conclude the parliamentary  phases of this uh before summer recess  so that which is a week on wednesday  exactly over the next couple of weeks we  want to to get this concluded and that  can absolutely be done uh through that  exhaustive ballot process you you can  hold several ballots in in one day if  you want to so the 1922 committee uh and  segregated brady will obviously be  working out what the rules should be  but i absolutely think yes it's uh it's  doable it's a tight timetable uh it  means people have a short period of time  to make their case uh but i'm here today  to make that case for grant chaps okay  um it does seem with all of the runners  and riders that there was an awful lot  of pent-up frustration as far as this  prime minister is concerned all of these  people now wanting his job why did you  stick by him  look i 